# Windows app design analysis and Ox integration

Research date: **2026-11-17**. Requested reference: [Design for Windows apps](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/). Product decision: **VISUAL STUDIO 2026 ENTERPRISE is the mandatory visual base for Ox**. [DESIGN.md](../../DESIGN.md) owns the adopted rules; this document owns source coverage, interpretation and transfer limits. The [decision](../decisions/windows-design-baseline.md) and [SDD feature](../../sdd/features/windows-design-baseline/spec.md) record adoption and documentary acceptance.

## Research boundary and reproducibility

The analysis covers every internal article enumerated by the official Windows design table of contents: **44 of 44 articles**, across every foundation family, with a disposition below. All 44 source files were fetched successfully (779,143 bytes). The MicrosoftDocs/windows-dev-docs `docs` revision observed was `a3812316d9d6678be4e526ffb4b1c4f3958d956d`. The [source inventory](../validation/windows-design-20261117/source-inventory.json) records exact URLs, byte counts and SHA-256 values; the [TOC](../validation/windows-design-20261117/source-toc.yml) defines the denominator. The external “Build Windows apps” entry is a development hub, outside that design-article denominator.

Method: inspect the requested public landing page and official linked guidance, acquire the pinned Microsoft documentation sources, read their design prose/tables and classify every TOC entry for Ox. The original tracking-query URL returned older indexed content; reopening the canonical URL returned a newer hub including Haptics, which is absent from the pinned TOC. Haptics is explicitly included in the supplemental set below. They are separately identified; a current source revision is not proof that all rendered Learn pages have identical bytes. Raw source dates also need not match a rendered page's last-updated date. Third-party search results were not used as design authorities.

Twelve additional controls/input/haptics sources were inspected for relevant interaction guidance (§ Supplemental controls and input); these do not silently change the 44-article denominator. Three official VS2026 screenshots were opened and visually inspected (§ Visual Studio reference). API examples were examined for platform applicability, not compiled or executed. Glyph catalogs were inspected for usage, fallback and licensing guidance, **not individually rendered or optically reviewed glyph by glyph**. Linked source figures and animated demonstrations were not all individually inspected. No claim of exhaustive traversal of every transitive API, repository, Fluent site, downloadable kit or Windows development manual is made. This is complete direct-TOC coverage plus a declared supporting set, with explicit applicability analysis; it is not a mirror of the entire evolving Microsoft site.

## Authority and application

The user selects the visual base. Microsoft describes its own products; Microsoft documentation cannot grant permissions or change the Ox architecture. Constitution, functional truth and current authorization retain precedence. DESIGN.md translates the selected base into an Ox contract. AGENTS routes contributors to it; SDD specifies affected behavior/evidence; reusable skills supply methods instead of becoming independent product-policy authorities.

Apply the **desktop IDE** character of VS2026 Enterprise: a quiet, compact shell; an explorer and contextual inspectors; a dominant tabbed artifact/editor; grouped tool windows; a restrained status strip; readable technical density; coherent command icons, focus and selection. The result remains agent-first, with tasks, execution, verification and evidence as first-class work areas. “Enterprise” identifies the requested edition and reference product. It does not prove an Enterprise-exclusive palette, layout or visual skin, and it does not adopt Enterprise features or subscriptions.

Retain the OX Catalisador identity, approved artwork provenance, IBM Plex typography and established semantic color roles. Those are deliberate local adaptations, not omissions. Their preservation does not make a generic dashboard equivalent to the selected IDE. Future visual proposals must show how their composition follows the base and explain each actual deviation. Existing UI is not retroactively declared conformant.

## Complete source-to-project matrix

“Adopt” below means an intended/documented requirement where represented in DESIGN.md. It never means the native UI has implemented or passed it. “Conditional” and “not applicable” entries retain their rationale so a later platform decision can revisit them.

| ID | Official article | Finding | Ox disposition |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| W01 | [Entry point](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/) | Hub for principles, guidance and tools; its links define the bounded design corpus. | Route visual work through DESIGN.md; this research supplies rationale, not an execution authority. |
| W02 | [Principles](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/design-principles) | Low effort, calm presentation, personalization, familiarity and coherence. | Make the active artifact primary, reuse predictable IDE commands and preserve task context across destinations. |
| W03 | [Guidelines overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/guidelines-overview) | Thirteen foundation families organize decisions beyond color alone. | Review each family below; do not treat a Fluent palette as a complete design system. |
| W04 | [Color](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/signature-experiences/color) | Neutral theme surfaces with restrained interaction accents and contrast-aware state. | Separate shell/editor/syntax/category/status tokens; never use OX blue or verification green as a universal fill. |
| W05 | [Commanding](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/basics/commanding-basics) | Choose actions around user tasks, expose frequent commands and give outcome feedback. | Keep run, cancel, inspect and verify contextually distinct; navigation must not start effects. Undo/confirmation follows actual consequences and authorization. |
| W06 | [Elevation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/signature-experiences/layering) | Base/content layers and purposeful overlap establish hierarchy. | Use flat docked panes and thin separators; reserve elevation for menus/dialogs. Source elevation numbers are not Iced shadow parameters. |
| W07 | [Geometry](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/signature-experiences/geometry) | Consistent rounding; joined edges differ from free-standing controls. | Use shared geometry tokens; joined tabs, trees and tool panes must remain compact. Windows 4/8px radii are reference values, not a wholesale token migration. |
| W08 | [Iconography](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/iconography/) | Application identity, command symbols and file metaphors have different jobs. | Retain OX artwork and one coherent native command family. Selection, focus and domain status need distinct cues and accessible names. |
| W09 | [App icons](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/iconography/app-icons) | Recognizable identity must survive the many places an OS displays it. | Preserve Catalisador/OX provenance; test actual small-size exports when packaging is authorized. This is not permission to replace the identity with a Microsoft mark. |
| W10 | [Icon design](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/iconography/app-icon-design) | Simple metaphor, balanced silhouette and restrained details improve small-size recognition. | Inspect OX at intended sizes/backgrounds; do not apply the illustrative app-icon gradients to every workbench command. |
| W11 | [Icon construction](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/iconography/app-icon-construction) | Export sizes, transparency and theme variants depend on the packaging target. | Windows ICO/AppList/Store assets are conditional future packaging work. Their filename matrix is not a Linux acceptance requirement. |
| W12 | [Asset generation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/iconography/visual-studio-asset-generation) | Manifest Designer can seed Windows package images but scaling still needs inspection. | Not applicable to current Rust/Iced packaging; do not add a Visual Studio build dependency or claim generated icons were checked. |
| W13 | [Legacy MDL2](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/iconography/segoe-ui-symbol-font) | Legacy symbol font uses private Unicode points and depends on the exact font. | Do not introduce MDL2 or bare PUA glyphs as Linux fallbacks; old glyph API examples do not supersede current icon provenance. |
| W14 | [Fluent icon font](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/iconography/segoe-fluent-icons-font) | Current Windows icon-font guidance includes size, mirroring and availability restrictions. | Use semantic names and licensed native equivalents. Font download access does not authorize shipping Segoe Fluent Icons on Linux. |
| W15 | [Layout overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/layout/) | Window size, resolution and orientation require flexible composition. | Keep a primary working area while adapting auxiliary panels; layout does not gain an acceptance verdict from a mockup. |
| W16 | [Silhouettes](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/basics/app-silhouette) | Menu, left-navigation and tab-oriented structures suit different tasks. | Adopt the VS2026 workbench silhouette: compact command chrome, explorers, document tabs, primary artifact and contextual tool panes. |
| W17 | [Title bar](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/basics/titlebar-design) | Window identification/management, drag regions, caption buttons and active state must coexist with commands. | Keep OS window behavior; 48-unit Ox chrome is not evidence of a custom title bar. Protect controls from drag hit regions and scaling overlap if later integrated. |
| W18 | [Content spacing](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/basics/content-basics) | Spacing, labels, headers and aligned controls form meaningful groups. | Reuse spacing roles and align tree/table/form rows; choose dense IDE composition over media-app margins. Never reduce essential text to repair overflow. |
| W19 | [Breakpoints](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/layout/screen-sizes-and-breakpoints-for-responsive-design) | Use available window space and logical dimensions, not monitor pixels. | Retain existing Ox 900/1100 adaptation until deliberately changed. Microsoft 640/1008 classes inform tests, not new automatic breakpoints or platform scope. |
| W20 | [Responsive techniques](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/layout/responsive-design) | Reposition, resize, reflow, disclose and adapt without losing the user task. | Collapse or stack secondary panes with a reachable restore path, valid focus and preserved context; avoid a permanently squeezed three-column layout. |
| W21 | [Materials overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/signature-experiences/materials) | Mica is a base backdrop; Acrylic suits transient context; Smoke communicates modality. | Carry surface hierarchy into opaque Iced surfaces. Do not simulate blur beneath essential text as a condition of visual fidelity. |
| W22 | [Acrylic](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/style/acrylic) | Translucency requires readability, bounded use and solid fallbacks. | Keep long-lived editor/explorer panes opaque. Any future effect needs contrast, transparency-preference and renderer/performance checks on the actual platform. |
| W23 | [Mica](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/style/mica) | Opaque theme/wallpaper material and active/inactive treatment organize long-lived windows. | Adopt the base/content relationship; actual Windows compositor APIs and Mica Alt remain conditional. A tinted Linux panel is not an implemented Mica backdrop. |
| W24 | [Motion overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/signature-experiences/motion) | Motion should connect related states and respond consistently to input. | No essential animation; existing no-animation default remains. A future transition must support orientation and remain interruptible without delaying commands. |
| W25 | [Timing/easing](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/motion/timing-and-easing) | Platform resources provide duration and acceleration references. | 83/167/250ms examples are not Ox latency budgets. Honor reduced motion and report actual timing measurements independently. |
| W26 | [Direction/gravity](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/motion/directionality-and-gravity) | Transitions should maintain a coherent direction and reversible mental model. | If movement is introduced, map entry/exit to actual navigation. Do not add bouncing panes or spatial metaphors that obscure evidence. |
| W27 | [Navigation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/basics/navigation-basics) | Consistent labels, shallow hierarchy and explicit peer/parent relationships support wayfinding. | Keep stable destination IDs, grouped categories and current location; do not turn all 27 destinations into unrelated dashboard cards or impose an arbitrary seven-item quota. |
| W28 | [Typography](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/signature-experiences/typography) | Readable hierarchy, appropriate scale, language coverage and fallback precede ornament. | Keep IBM Plex UI/Mono and existing size roles. Segoe UI Variable, large display sizes and prose-line recommendations are not mandatory editor settings. |
| W29 | [Usability overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/usability/) | Accessibility, language, preferences and help jointly determine usability. | Include these questions in changed-surface review; decorative parity alone cannot approve a native journey. |
| W30 | [Accessibility overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/accessibility/accessibility-overview) | Programmatic roles/names, keyboard access, scaling, contrast and assistive technology require implementation and testing. | Define native semantics, focus order and state announcements; validate the actual Iced/platform bridge. WinUI automation peers are not inherited by Iced. |
| W31 | [Inclusive software](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/accessibility/designing-inclusive-software) | Account for permanent, temporary and situational needs early and with real users. | Keep critical journeys usable with keyboard/zoom and multiple state cues. User research, screen-reader success and compliance remain unclaimed without observation. |
| W32 | [Settings](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/app-settings/guidelines-for-app-settings) | Organize stable preferences with clear scope, immediate feedback and reasons for disabled choices. | Use a VS-like searchable category/content composition when implemented; preserve the requested upper-right cog. Session and persisted settings must remain distinguishable. |
| W33 | [Bidirectional text](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/globalizing/design-for-bidi-text) | UI flow, mixed-script content and punctuation need separate handling. | pt-BR remains the interface language; test Unicode paths/content. Do not mirror code/artwork or alter stored path/source bytes to fix display direction. |
| W34 | [Help principles](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/in-app-help/guidelines-for-app-help) | Help should resolve the current issue without compensating for undiscoverable basics. | Use contextual, concise recovery guidance next to the failed operation. Ordinary product flows should not expose governance internals. |
| W35 | [Instructional UI](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/in-app-help/instructional-ui) | Teach non-obvious capabilities briefly without covering work or forcing a tour. | Disclose optional hints for actual interactions only; unavailable capabilities are not onboarding promises. Preserve keyboard access and dismissal. |
| W36 | [In-app help](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/in-app-help/in-app-help) | User-requested concise help is suitable for immediate reference. | Provide local descriptions of real controls and supported recovery; do not require remote documentation to discover primary commands. |
| W37 | [External help](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/in-app-help/external-help) | Long tutorials belong in organized external material with contextual entry points. | Label external navigation and preserve the work context; source research does not authorize opening browsers or transmitting project data from the product. |
| W38 | [Windows Widgets](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/widgets/) | Widgets Board providers and Adaptive Cards are separate OS integrations. | Not applicable to current desktop scope; an Iced widget is not a Windows Widget. Adopt only the general idea of focused, truthful summaries. |
| W39 | [Widget fundamentals](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/widgets/widgets-design-fundamentals) | Three sizes, themes, attribution and glanceable content have host-specific constraints. | Do not import touchpoint quotas, card dimensions or Adaptive Cards into the IDE. Keep genuine source/freshness information in any summary. |
| W40 | [Widget states](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/widgets/widgets-states-and-ui) | Host and provider own different error, cached, signed-out and customization states. | Useful analogy for explicit unavailable/stale data; Windows provider lifecycle and preview APIs remain outside scope, without fabricated sign-in or network flows. |
| W41 | [Widget interactions](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/widgets/widgets-interaction-design) | Host-specific navigation, pagination and dismissal constrain interactions. | Do not apply its ban on scrolling or second-level views to an IDE. Deep-link capability would require separate authorization and implementation. |
| W42 | [Widget picker](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/widgets/widgets-picker-integration) | Preview images, locale fallback and package manifest metadata are OS-specific. | Not applicable to current Ox assets/acceptance. A later Widgets integration must recheck these requirements and current APIs. |
| W43 | [Writing](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/style/writing-style) | Action-led, concise, helpful text and specific recovery reduce ambiguity. | Use pt-BR sentence case, explicit verbs and factual errors. Keep paths/identifiers intact; translate intent rather than English contraction rules. |
| W44 | [Resources](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/downloads/) | Fonts, Figma kits, Gallery and Store kits support design but have their own licenses/tooling. | Optional inspection aids; no font download, kit installation, runtime dependency or Store publication is adopted by this research. |

## Visual Studio reference

The [VS2026 release notes](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/releases/2026/release-notes#new-look-and-feel) document the Fluent refresh, editor appearance separated from the shell, updated icons and modern settings. The current consulted page also includes subsequent releases; the named initial refresh section is the visual reference, not a moving promise to reproduce every later feature. The [official product download page](https://visualstudio.microsoft.com/downloads/) identifies the Enterprise edition; selection does not imply acquisition or licensing for this task.

[Microsoft's theme article](https://devblogs.microsoft.com/visualstudio/make-visual-studio-look-the-way-you-want/) explains per-theme semantic color customization, including separation between shell, tabs and headers. Its examples are user customization examples; no hex values from that article are adopted as official Enterprise defaults. The product has multiple themes. The requested edition therefore defines composition and interaction language rather than one purported universal screenshot palette.

Official reference images actually inspected (source identity in the [image inventory](../validation/windows-design-20261117/image-inventory.json)):

| Image | Observed visual evidence | Transfer to Ox and limits |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Editor appearance](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/releases/2026/media/18.0/fluent-editor.png) | Narrow top command rows; left explorer; dominant central document tabs/editor; right contextual panel; bottom tools/status. The warm shell and dark editor are visibly different. A settings overlay is open. | Supports distinct shell/editor roles and docked IDE structure. Screenshot annotation/preview markers and the sample Copilot content are not product UI to copy; exact dimensions were not measured. |
| [Explorer icons](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/releases/2026/media/18.0/fluent-ui-icons.png) | Compact tree indentation, small semantic icons, clear chevrons, a search field, tool commands and a visible selected row. | Guides readable, restrained hierarchy and a selected marker distinct from keyboard focus. It does not prove any of these interactions or a matching hierarchy engine exists in Ox. |
| [Modern settings](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/releases/2026/media/18.0/usx-full.png) | Search/filter header, category tree, section headings, aligned inputs and explanatory text on a neutral dark background. | Guides settings information structure and progressive disclosure when supported. No settings storage, search service, sync or account integration is implemented by adopting a screenshot. |

These are public VS2026 reference images, not captures of an installed Enterprise instance. No Enterprise-specific appearance difference was established. No native Visual Studio session or side-by-side Ox parity measurement was performed. Images were used as references and are not redistributed as Ox assets.

## Supplemental controls and input

The current [controls hub](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/develop/ui/controls/) separates basic input, collections, dialogs, forms, media, menus, navigation, people/pickers, scrolling, status, text and title bars. This breadth informs component selection; adopting an IDE base does not require every control. The [input hub](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/develop/input/) distinguishes mouse, keyboard, touch, pen and other devices; “automatic” WinUI behavior does not transfer to Iced. Older design URLs for these hubs redirect to `develop/`.

| Supporting source | Relevant finding and Ox application |
| --- | --- |
| [Haptics design](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/design/signature-experiences/haptics) | Reinforce meaningful user interactions consistently with optional tactile feedback. Respect off/reduced preferences and preserve visible outcome cues. Conditional future device/platform work; no vibration is implemented or required for current Linux acceptance. |
| [Haptics implementation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/develop/input/haptics) | Check API/device availability, stop feedback when interaction ends and tolerate unsupported delivery. Windows APIs and suggested debounce/latency examples are not automatically adopted in Iced or as Ox performance budgets. |
| [Tree view](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/develop/ui/controls/tree-view) | Indentation, chevrons and item icons expose hierarchy. Distinguish expand, select and invoke; lazy nodes and selection scope require explicit state. A painted tree or directionally drawn Cargo map does not implement filesystem/semantic discovery. |
| [Dialogs](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/develop/ui/controls/dialogs-and-flyouts/dialogs) | Reserve modality for decisions that need it; explicit action labels and a safe dismiss path preserve clarity. Bind any confirmed effect to actual authorization; Escape or closing must not imply consent. |
| [Flyouts](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/develop/ui/controls/dialogs-and-flyouts/flyouts) | Anchor transient content to its invoker; define focus restoration and outside-click dismissal. A dismiss click must not activate a destructive underlying control. Tooltips remain supplementary descriptions, not decision dialogs. |
| [Forms](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/develop/ui/controls/forms) | Distinguish immediate preferences from explicit submission; associate labels/errors with controls, preserve input on failure and use predictable reading/tab order. Source 80px fields and 24/48px spacing are examples, unsuitable as blanket IDE dimensions. |
| [Menus/context menus](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/develop/ui/controls/menus-and-context-menus) | Frequent actions need discoverable access; secondary commands can attach to a target. Context action identity must include its target, and keyboard access must reach the same available command. |
| [Command bar](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/develop/ui/controls/command-bar) | Rank commands by task importance; adapt secondary actions to overflow while preserving a stable location and labels. A compact workbench cannot silently drop a critical run/cancel/verify action on resize. |
| [Focus navigation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/develop/input/focus-navigation) | Separate traversal among groups from movement within composites. Restore meaningful focus after navigation, overlay dismissal and pane changes; hidden controls must not remain in traversal. Verify actual widget behavior. |
| [Keyboard accelerators](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/develop/input/keyboard-accelerators) | Shortcuts need discoverability, scope and collision handling; disabled commands must remain unavailable through shortcuts. Preserve text-editing keys and window-manager bindings; copying VS shortcuts is not proof of platform compatibility. |

The supporting set (ten pinned files including both hubs, plus two live Haptics articles) and exact hashes are retained in [supplemental inventory](../validation/windows-design-20261117/supplemental-source-inventory.json). Haptics public repository paths returned HTTP 404 at the pinned revision, so those two entries identify live Learn HTML bytes and consultation date instead of claiming a pinned source commit. Only relevant design/interaction sections were analyzed; this is not an exhaustive API review. No sample was compiled and no control or package was installed. A guessed `/design/patterns/` page was unavailable; it was not used as a source. Patterns were examined through the actual controls hub instead.

## Decisions where direct transplantation would fail

| Topic | Source context or conflict | Adopted treatment |
| --- | --- | --- |
| IDE versus a general Windows app | Windows guidance includes content-heavy/media/settings apps as well as utilities. | VS2026 Enterprise controls the visual silhouette; Windows principles refine usability. Do not flatten the IDE into oversized cards or a mobile-style page stack. |
| Accent and color state | OS accent, syntax highlighting and domain status serve different meanings. | OX identity/accent, action affordance, navigation selection, syntax, evidence result and data categories use distinct semantic roles. A purple selection is not an approved task. |
| Rounding and layer depth | Windows radius/elevation examples describe specific controls/compositor behavior. | Reuse Ox tokens and restrained, purpose-specific variants; record intentional changes rather than rewriting locked colors or adopting giant rounded pane cards. |
| Type ramp | Windows recommends Segoe; desktop source/editor content has different reading needs. | IBM Plex remains the native baseline. Apply hierarchy and scaling principles without silently importing Segoe/Consolas or constraining code to prose line lengths. |
| Font distribution | The Segoe Fluent Icons article explicitly restricts shipping the font to another platform. | Keep licensed existing assets/native symbols. Asset reuse needs its own provenance/license review; copying PUA code points is not portability. |
| Settings entry point | Generic guidance suggests a final/bottom navigation item; Ox has a specific upper-right cog instruction. | Retain the explicit cog placement and discoverability. Use coherent category/content layout within settings; avoid a second competing preferences flow. |
| Contrast guidance | Inclusive-software prose mentions 5:1; widget guidance cites 4.5:1 for normal text. Some text truncation advice is also context-dependent. | The explicit scoped WCAG 2.2 targets in DESIGN.md remain the acceptance reference: 4.5:1 normal text, 3:1 qualifying large text/essential nontext. 5:1 can be extra margin. Neither statement establishes legal conformance. Preserve essential identifiers rather than blindly clip them. |
| Logical units and adaptation | Microsoft uses effective pixels and device classes; Iced uses its own logical layout/scale. | Record window dimensions and scale separately, retain actual Ox thresholds until changed, test narrow/large/localized layouts on the target renderer. No automatic transfer of XAML scaling is assumed. |
| Backdrops and animation | Native Windows materials/motion have OS support and preference fallbacks. | Opaque, low-motion equivalents satisfy current cross-platform intent; a future native backdrop requires separate implementation and evidence. No measured speed/energy claim follows from appearance. |
| Settings persistence | Microsoft examples persist or roam preferences; Ox has narrowly scoped local project history and session preferences. | State scope honestly. This task does not select a database, add sync or broaden general persistence. |
| Windows Widgets and packaging | Adaptive Cards/Widgets Board, MSIX, manifest assets and Store submission target Windows. | Conditional future platform work; exclude from current Linux/Iced acceptance with reasons rather than pretending the concepts are implemented. |
| Source age and API migration | The corpus contains older UWP examples, preview widget notices, redirects and new guidance together. | Recheck actual platform/framework/version before implementing. The guidance can inform design without adopting legacy APIs, archived samples or unsupported host guarantees. |

## Integration by Ox surface

| Ox surface/contract | Required design application | Evidence needed for a future native change |
| --- | --- | --- |
| S01/S02 project entry | IDE start environment; compact OX identity; clear new/open/clone availability; real recent paths in the primary area; a docked, readable form. | Native captures and keyboard/error journeys for opening/creating projects; no invented clone effect. |
| W00 artifact/editor and explorer | Primary tabbed working canvas; hierarchy, selection, dirty/error/read-only state and source context; restrained contextual inspectors. | Same data/state/theme/scale comparison; tab/focus/overflow journeys; real file editing tested separately from demonstration. |
| Task execution, decisions and review | Contextual commands, consequences and progress; distinguish requested/running/result/verification/acceptance; preserve failure evidence. | Actual state transitions and rejected/unavailable actions, including keyboard, cancellation and late results. |
| Agents, providers, tools and SDD/skills | Dense trees/tables with stable labels and inspectable scope/source; distinguish definitions, instances and capability availability. | Real versus demo data identified; portable SDD files and permission boundaries remain authoritative. |
| Architecture/observability | VS-like tools support inspecting source-bound structures and metrics. | Provenance/freshness/units and empty/unavailable cases; no graph-engine or measured-performance claim from painted data. |
| S13/S14 preferences | Shared cog, searchable/category-based information structure where implemented, clear theme/density/scope and progressive details. | Test changed controls, persistence boundaries, theme/state contrast and focus. Search or system-theme support remains planned unless implemented. |
| Shared widgets/overlays | Consistent roles, tooltips, visible focus, disabled reasons, aligned inputs and predictable safe dismissal. | Contrast/rendering plus actual pointer/keyboard/assistive technology observations as applicable. |

Surface identifiers and implementation truth remain in [surfaces](surfaces.md), [components](components.md) and [reference coverage](reference-coverage.md). This matrix constrains future work and reviews; it does not initiate implementation in any of them.

## Acceptance and maintenance

For each affected visual task, SDD should record the reference revision/theme, chosen surfaces/states, local adaptations, actual input/content, logical size/scale, oracle and limitations. Compare the same task and data. Native rendering, pointer/keyboard operation, assistive technology and functional effects require distinct observations. Documentation/link checks establish documentary integrity only; reference screenshots do not prove Ox parity, usability, accessibility, performance or product acceptance.

This integration changes documentation and skill guidance. It does not modify Rust widgets, dependencies, theme implementations or asset licenses. Existing F0/native evidence stays bound to its original inputs; dependent current evidence must be reevaluated instead of rehashed to look fresh. Current acceptance and actual checks belong in [the feature convergence record](../../sdd/features/windows-design-baseline/converge.md).

Maintain the analysis with DESIGN.md when the selected visual base changes, Microsoft moves/deprecates a relevant pattern, a new target platform is authorized, or native evidence reveals a mismatch. Preserve source identity and prior observations; extend coverage for the actual changed question. Do not require a network reread of the entire portal for every local visual repair.
